## WHO response
WHO supports countries and stakeholders to implement the recommended actions by:
- developing global policies, guidelines and technical guidance based on the best available evidence;
- supporting countries to strengthen national policies, programmes and multisectoral action to promote physical activity;
- providing practical tools, training and capacity-building resources to support implementation across settings and populations;
- convening and coordinating partners across sectors, including health, sport, transport, education and urban planning, to create environments that support active lives; and
- strengthening surveillance, monitoring progress towards global targets, and advancing the evidence base, including through innovations such as wearable technologies and international research collaborations.

Led by WHO and the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations (FAO), the UN Decade of Action on Nutrition calls for policy action across 6 key areas:
- creating sustainable, resilient food systems for healthy diets;
- providing social protection and nutrition-related education for all;
- aligning health systems to nutrition needs, and providing universal coverage of essential nutrition interventions;
- ensuring that trade and investment policies improve nutrition;
- building safe and supportive environments for nutrition at all ages; and
- strengthening and promoting nutrition governance and accountability, everywhere.

Goals.
The UN Decade of Healthy Ageing (2021–2030) seeks to reduce health inequities and improve the lives of older people, their families and communities through collective action in four areas: changing how we think, feel and act towards age and ageism;
 developing communities in ways that foster the abilities of older people; delivering person-centred integrated care and primary health services responsive to older people; and providing older people who need it with access to quality long-term care.
